  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The marginal distribution $P(x)$ can be obtained by integrating the conditional distribution $P(x|y)$ with respect to $y$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'So the integral of conditional density, P of y given x with respect to y. What will it result to? Well, with respect to y, this is just probability density function. And the integral of probability density function always results in one.' This is a bit confusing because it says 'P of y given x' but the context suggests it's $P(x|y)$. However, the subsequent explanation clarifies that the integral of $P(x|y)$ over $y$ gives $P(x)$, which is the marginal distribution. The timestamped segment [00:09:08\u201300:10:00] supports this claim."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginal distribution $P(x)$ is equal to the expectation of $P(x|y)$ over all possible values of $y$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'So it is the same as to say that this is expectation with respect to y over all conditional distributions.' The timestamped segment [00:10:46\u201300:11:17] explicitly mentions this interpretation. The claim is supported by the ASR's description of the expectation interpretation."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginal distribution $P(x)$ can be expressed as $\\int P(x|y) P(y) \\, dy$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'one equals P of x given y times P of y' and then explains the derivation. The timestamped segment [00:10:02\u201300:10:46] shows the derivation leading to $P(x) = \\int P(x|y) P(y) \\, dy$. This is a direct mathematical expression from the ASR."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginal distribution $P(x)$ is obtained by averaging the conditional distributions $P(x|y)$ over all $y$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'we take all different conditional distributions on x given different y's, we average them out with respect to y and we obtain unconditional distribution on x.' The timestamped segment [00:10:46\u201300:11:17] supports this claim."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The concept of marginalization extends to multiple variables, where the marginal distribution over $x_1, ..., x_k$ can be obtained by integrating out the remaining variables.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'by induction it's straightforward to prove that this goes through for arbitrary number of random variables so you have say distribution over x and x1 over x1 xn and we would like to get distribution over x1 xk we can easily do it just by taking joint distribution and integrating out remaining variables' The timestamped segment [00:11:49\u201300:12:44] supports this claim."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginal distribution $P(x)$ is equivalent to integrating the joint distribution $P(x, y)$ over $y$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'we can also, if we remember the product rule, we can also note that this is the same as to say that this is just a joint distribution where y was integrated out.' The timestamped segment [00:11:17\u201300:11:49] supports this claim."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The integral of the conditional distribution $P(x|y)$ with respect to $y$ results in 1.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'with respect to y, this is just probability density function. And the integral of probability density function always results in one.' The timestamped segment [00:09:08\u201300:10:00] supports this claim."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginalization process for multiple variables is proven by induction.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'by induction it's straightforward to prove that this goes through for arbitrary number of random variables' The timestamped segment [00:11:49\u201300:12:44] supports this claim."
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
